    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3053": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty",
      "description": "Part-Time Faculty - Accounting, Spring 2027 & Fall 2027 - UMS Careers Skip to main content. sitemap Profile Sign Out View More Jobs Part-Time Faculty - Accounting, Spring 2027 & Fall 2027 Portland, ME, United States and 2 more (On-site) Job Info Job Identification 3053 Job Category Temporary Posting Date 09/11/2026, 09:55 AM Locations P.O. Box 9300, Portland, ME, 04104, US (On-site) Business Unit University of Southern Maine Job Schedule Part time Regular or Temporary Temporary Department USM Accounting and Finance Bargaining Unit Maine Part-Time Faculty Association (PATFA) Job Description The Accounting and Finance Department at the University of Southern Maine is seeking part-time instructor applicants for its upcoming Spring 2027 and Fall 2027 semesters. We are seeking instructors to teach the following courses : Managerial Accounting Information for Decision Making (ACC 211) (Spring 2027, Online) - This course will provide students with the opportunity to learn basic concepts and accounting systems involved in the use of managerial accounting information in planning and control decisions in organizations. Basic concepts include different types of costs (e.g., direct, indirect, fixed, variable, and relevant costs). Basic accounting systems include systems for cost allocation (e.g., job-order costing, activity-based costing), planning (e.g., cost-volume-profit analysis, master budget), and control (e.g., flexible budgets, variance analysis, responsibility accounting, performance measurement). Auditing and Assurance (ACC 410) (Spring 2027, In Person) – This course examines the public accounting profession, auditing standards, and professional ethics. The course explores the process by which an auditor forms an opinion as to the “fairness of presentation” of financial statements, giving an overview of audit evidence and audit evidence accumulation methodology. The course exposes students both to the demand for and supply of the profession’s flagship service, financial statement audits and to the nature of the value-added assurance and attestation services decision-makers demand in the information age. The course illustrates real companies, links class discussion and assignments to student skills, and encourages unstructured problem-solving. This course provides an opportunity for students to study auditing concepts and theory at an advanced level by examining several issues, with extensive reading from the auditing research literature, in addition to the textbook material. Financial Accounting Information for Decision Making (ACC 210) (Fall 2027, In Person, Two Sections) – This course is designed to help students appreciate the role of accountants in providing information helpful to the decisions of investors, creditors, government regulators, and others, and how that information can be used. Emphasis is on understanding the meaning and value of the balance sheet, income statement, and statement of cash flows. The role of the auditor, internal controls, and ethical issues are examined. The annual report is used to explore how corporations apply accounting principles in presentations to the public. Financial Reporting I (ACC 301) (Fall 2027, In Person) - An examination of the conceptual framework, the primary financial statements, and the methods and rationale for recording and reporting assets. Emphasis is on the effect of present and potential economic events on the financial statements. The course discusses the advantages, limitations and deficiencies associated with generally accepted principles in connection with presenting decision-useful information. We are seeking student-focused instructors who will be responsible for the full delivery and grading of the course. All Special Education programs are fully online with a combination of synchronous and asynchronous courses.  Synchronous sessions meet using the Zoom conferencing platform and meet in the late afternoons and evenings so that those currently working in schools can attend.  Responsibilities include developing the course, teaching, assessing student performance, and meeting with students as needed. The instructor will be provided with the course learning objectives, program competencies, and suggested topical outline and will be given a previous course syllabus available for reference. Part-time faculty members are paid on a credit hour basis based on prior teaching experience, as determined by the Part-Time Faculty Association of Maine (PATFA), an affiliate of the national American Federation of Teachers (AFT) union. To view the Credit Hour Rates for AY 2026, please refer to the PATFA Contract . Eligible part-time faculty members may elect certain benefits provided by the University of Maine System.  In addition, The Center for Academic Innovation provides learning design consulting and faculty development opportunities to help faculty to create engaging programs, courses and lesson plans grounded in evidence-based best practices for every modality. About the University of Southern Maine The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond.  USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement.  We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Master's or PhD in accounting or related field. For course ACC 410 (Auditing and Assurance) a CPA is required. Previous teaching experience at the university or college level is preferred but not required. To apply , click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position. Curriculum vitae or resume Finalist candidates will be asked for contact information for three professional references. We will begin reviewing applications immediately.  These positions will remain open until filled. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate(s). The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. Apply Now Accreditation Privacy Policy Freedom of Information Act Accessibility Title IX © 2026 University of Maine System University Students Faculty & Staff Trustees Data & Policy I am an employee Are You Still With Us? It seems you've been gone for a while. For security reasons we will end your session automatically in 03:00 unless you would like to continue working. End Session Continue Working Work Summary This summary is generated by AI Assist. Click inside the summary text box to make changes as necessary. 1000 Required Discard Add Summary Page Part-Time Faculty - Accounting, Spring 2027 & Fall 2027 - UMS Careers loaded"
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3089": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Extension Professor, PFAS and Environmental Contaminants Analytical Specialist, and PFAS Lab Director",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Extension Professor, PFAS and Environmental Contaminants Analytical Specialist, and PFAS Lab Director - UMS Careers Skip to main content. sitemap Profile Sign Out View More Jobs Assistant or Associate Extension Professor, PFAS and Environmental Contaminants Analytical Specialist, and PFAS Lab Director Orono, ME, United States (On-site) Job Info Job Identification 3089 Posting Date 09/11/2026, 05:31 AM Locations College Avenue, Orono, ME, 04469, US (On-site) Business Unit University of Maine Job Schedule Full time Regular or Temporary Regular Department UM Cooperative Extension Bargaining Unit Associated Faculties of the University of Maine System (AFUM) Job Description The University of Maine Cooperative Extension is seeking an accomplished scientist and collaborative leader to establish and grow the University's PFAS analytical and Extension program. This is a unique opportunity to build research capacity, direct a state-of-the-art analytical laboratory, and work alongside agricultural producers, researchers, and policymakers to address the evolving challenges of PFAS contamination in Maine. The successful candidate will combine scientific excellence with a commitment to public engagement, using applied research and Extension education to translate complex environmental science into practical solutions that strengthen Maine's agricultural and food systems. Typical hiring range for this position is $85,000 to $90,000 commensurate with qualifications and experience. Complete Job Description Who We Are: The University of Maine Cooperative Extension has been a vital resource for over 100 years, connecting communities with university research to improve lives across the state. Our focus areas include the Maine Food System and Positive Youth Development through 4-H programs, delivered by a network of experts based in county offices statewide. We pride ourselves on being an equitable, sustainable, and enjoyable workplace, and we welcome diverse perspectives and experiences. We also believe in work and life balance and offer benefits to support you. Learn more about our mission and work at extension.umaine.edu . The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu. The University of Maine System provides a highly competitive benefits package that includes: holidays, vacation and sick time; health, dental and vision insurance; life insurance; short and long term disability insurance; a tuition waiver program for employee and eligible dependents; a 403(b) retirement plan; and more. Knowledge and Skill Qualifications We Are Looking For : Required: These are required knowledge and skills so be sure that you demonstrate how you meet each one in your resume or cover letter. Doctorate degree in the area of, Analytical Chemistry, Environmental Chemistry, Chemistry, Soil Science, Environmental Science, or a closely related field, by date of hire. Approximately 5 years of experience in PFAS analysis, pesticide residue analysis, or similar work. Demonstrated experience with LC-MS/MS analysis, data interpretation, troubleshooting, instrument maintenance, and vendor/service coordination. Demonstrated experience managing annual laboratory budgets, fiscal-year planning, recharge/service-center operations, procurement, service contracts, cost recovery, and financial reporting. Documented record of conducting high-quality, applied scientific research, demonstrated by publication in peer-reviewed journals and/or popular press. Experience supervising, training, and evaluating technical staff, research staff, students, postdoctoral researchers, and/or laboratory analysts. Experience in writing and maintaining standard operating procedures Experience analyzing PFAS or trace organic contaminants in environmental and/or agricultural matrices such as drinking water, groundwater, wastewater, soil, biosolids, sediment, plant tissue, forage, food crops, animal tissue, or related samples. Knowledge of current and emerging issues, practices, and trends in the PFAS analytical methods field. Demonstrated experience in managing the financial aspects of a fee-for-service lab and creating a sustainable annual budget. Demonstrated computer and educational technology skills including creating and maintaining analytical database systems. Effective verbal and written communication skills with the ability to articulate clear goals. Demonstrated experience with applied research and community needs assessments. Excellent interpersonal, technical writing, and public communication skills. Ability to creatively solve problems and manage conflicts within groups. Preferred: These are preferred knowledge and skills we are looking for but do not exclude you from the job if you don’t have this experience. Experience working in an accredited, regulated, service-center, or compliance-driven analytical laboratory. Experience with laboratory information management systems, sample tracking software, electronic data deliverables, or analytical data management platforms. Experience with automated SPE systems, automated extraction systems, LC-MS/MS troubleshooting, instrument maintenance, and vendor/service coordination. Experience communicating PFAS analytical results to non-specialist audiences, including farmers, agency partners, community members, and decision makers. Experience building partnerships with state agencies, federal agencies, agricultural organizations, commodity groups, or affected communities. Experience with Cooperative Extension educational program development, delivery and evaluation. Experience in grant writing and the financial management of grant funds. Experience working with diverse audiences; knowledge of diversity, equity and inclusion; and understanding of equal opportunity, affirmative action and civil rights policies. Successful supervisory experience. Proficiency with computer programs such as Microsoft Excel, Word and PowerPoint, as well as Google Drive and document management software. Benefits: Benefits are an important part of your total compensation. The University of Maine System (UMS) strives to provide high-quality, affordable, family-friendly benefit options to meet the needs of our diverse workforce. We offer an excellent benefits package that includes paid time off (holidays, vacation and sick), health, dental, vision and life insurance, short- and long-term disability insurance, a tuition waiver program for eligible employees and dependents, a 403(b) retirement plan and more. Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 pm on October 11th, 2026. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening. In complying with the letter and spirit of applicable laws and pursuing its own goals of diversity, the University of Maine System does not discriminate on the grounds of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability physical or mental, genetic information, or veterans or military status in employment, education, and all other programs and activities. The University provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding non-discrimination policies: Director of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services, 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, University of Maine, Orono, ME  04469-5713, 207.581.1226, TTY 711 (Maine Relay System). Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report. If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. Apply Now Accreditation Privacy Policy Freedom of Information Act Accessibility Title IX © 2026 University of Maine System University Students Faculty & Staff Trustees Data & Policy I am an employee Are You Still With Us? It seems you've been gone for a while. For security reasons we will end your session automatically in 03:00 unless you would like to continue working. End Session Continue Working Work Summary This summary is generated by AI Assist. Click inside the summary text box to make changes as necessary. 1000 Required Discard Add Summary Page Assistant or Associate Extension Professor, PFAS and Environmental Contaminants Analytical Specialist, and PFAS Lab Director - UMS Careers loaded"
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3126": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor of Nursing",
      "description": "Associate Professor or Professor of Nursing - UMS Careers Skip to main content. sitemap Profile Sign Out View More Jobs Associate Professor or Professor of Nursing Orono, ME, United States (Hybrid) Job Info Job Identification 3126 Posting Date 09/11/2026, 05:27 AM Locations College Avenue, Orono, ME, 04469, US (Hybrid) Business Unit University of Maine Job Schedule Full time Regular or Temporary Regular Department UM School of Nursing Bargaining Unit Associated Faculties of the University of Maine System (AFUM) Job Description The School of Nursing at the University of Maine seeks applicants for a full-time, academic-year, tenure-track, Associate/Full Professor position.  The initial appointment for this position will be to serve as the PI on an existing Department of Education Grant to launch New England’s first PhD program in Nursing. The successful candidate will provide academic leadership for the development and implementation of the program. The faculty member will ensure the quality and alignment of the PhD curriculum, support faculty teaching within the program, recruit and mentor doctoral students, and develop partnerships with healthcare organizations and institutions of higher education. The position will also advance the School of Nursing’s research mission through an active program of scholarship, grant development, research dissemination, and doctoral-student mentorship. The faculty member will teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned and contribute service to the School, University, profession, and public. At the conclusion of the DOE grant, the teaching, research, and administrative responsibilities will be negotiated based on ongoing need. Typical hiring range for this position is $80,000 to $95,000 commensurate with qualifications and experience. PhD program leadership throughout the funded Department of Education grant In collaboration with the Associate Director of Graduate Programs and the SON Director: Provide academic leadership for Northern New England’s first PhD program in Nursing. Guide the implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the PhD program. Ensure that courses and curricula align with program outcomes, University requirements, and applicable professional and accreditation standards. Facilitate collaboration among PhD faculty to promote cohesive, rigorous, and high-quality course design and instruction. Coordinate the program’s required immersion experiences. Recruit prospective PhD students and support activities designed to promote enrollment and retention. Meet with prospective students and provide information about the program, admissions process, curriculum, and career pathways. Develop partnerships with healthcare organizations, academic institutions, and other stakeholders to promote the PhD program and expand opportunities for collaboration. Seek internal and external funding to support PhD students, research, and program development. Ongoing responsibilities Teaching and student mentorship Teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned. Develop and deliver high-quality, evidence-informed instruction across appropriate teaching modalities. Advise and mentor doctoral students throughout their programs of study. Mentor students in research development, scholarly writing, dissemination, and grant preparation. Chair or serve on dissertation committees, as assigned. Participate in the assessment of student learning and ongoing evaluation of courses and curricula. Preference will be given to candidates qualified to teach: Measurement in Health and Behavioral Science Research Methods PhD Dissertation Seminar Dissertation Research Research and scholarship Maintain an active and sustained research agenda within the faculty member’s area of expertise. Pursue internal and external funding to support research, doctoral education, and student scholarship. Disseminate scholarly work through peer-reviewed publications and presentations at regional, national, and international conferences. Collaborate with faculty, students, healthcare organizations, and other partners on research and scholarly initiatives. Engage doctoral students in research, grant development, publication, and scholarly dissemination. Service Serve on School of Nursing and University committees. Contribute service to the nursing profession, healthcare community, and public. Participate in student recruitment, program events, commencement, and other School and University activities. Support the School’s academic, research, accreditation, and strategic priorities. Work location and travel expectations The successful candidate may reside anywhere in Maine. The position requires regular remote engagement and attendance at designated on-campus activities, including commencement, PhD immersion experiences, and other required meetings or program events. Travel throughout Maine and, when necessary, the broader region will be required for student recruitment, partnership development, and meetings with prospective students, healthcare organizations, and institutions of higher education. University-approved workspace will be provided near the employee’s primary residence, subject to institutional policies and availability. Faculty workload This is a tenure-track faculty appointment with workload allocated among teaching, research, and service. At the conclusion of the DOE grant in 2027, the teaching, research, and administrative responsibilities will be negotiated based on ongoing need.  A full teaching load is equivalent to 12 teaching credits per semester. Teaching release for approved program leadership, research, clinical practice, or service responsibilities is determined by the school director in accordance with institutional policies and available resources. About the University: The University of Maine is a community of more than 11,900 undergraduate and graduate students, and 2,500 employees located on the Orono campus, the regional campus in Machias, and throughout the state. UMaine is a land, sea and space grant university, and maintains a leadership role as the University of Maine System's flagship institution. UMaine is the state's public research university and a Carnegie R1 top-tier research institution, dedicated to providing excellent teaching, research and service for Maine, the nation and the world. More information about UMaine is at umaine.edu . The University of Maine offers a wide range of benefits for employees including, but not limited to, tuition benefits (employee and dependent), comprehensive insurance coverage including medical, dental, vision, life insurance, and short and long term disability as well as retirement plan options. UMaine is located in beautiful Central Maine. Many employees report that a primary reason for choosing to come to UMaine is quality of life. Numerous cultural activities, excellent public schools, safe neighborhoods, high quality medical care, little traffic, and a reasonable cost of living make the greater Bangor area a wonderful place to live. Visit the Maine Office of Tourism to learn more about what the Bangor region has to offer. Qualifications: Required: Earned PhD in Nursing from an accredited institution. Active, unrestricted registered nurse license in the State of Maine. Record of peer-reviewed scholarly publications and professional presentations commensurate with appointment at the rank of associate professor or professor. Demonstrated record of developing and sustaining an independent program of research following completion of the PhD. Experience mentoring PhD students through the dissertation process, including research development, proposal preparation, study implementation, scholarly writing, and dissertation completion. Demonstrated success pursuing or securing internal or external research funding. Knowledge of doctoral nursing education, curriculum development, program evaluation, and continuous quality improvement. Evidence of effective academic leadership and collaboration with faculty, students, healthcare organizations, and institutional partners. Strong written, interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills. Commitment to excellence in teaching, research, student mentorship, and academic service. Preferred: Experience leading or developing a PhD program in nursing. Experience chairing PhD dissertation committees. Record of externally funded research. Experience developing partnerships with healthcare organizations and institutions of higher education. Expertise teaching one or more of the following: Measurement in Health and Behavioral Science Research Methods PhD Dissertation Seminar Dissertation Research Other Information: To be considered for this position you will need to “Apply” and upload the documentation listed below: 1.) a cover letter which describes your experience, interests, and suitability for the position 2.) a resume/curriculum vitae Candidates selected to proceed to the final stages of the search process will be requested to provide a list of names and contact information for references. Incomplete application materials cannot be considered. Materials received after the initial review date will be reviewed at the discretion of the University. For full consideration, materials must be submitted by 4:30 p.m. EST on October 12, 2026. The successful applicant is subject to appropriate background screening. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator at www.maine.edu/title-ix/ . Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish and distribute an Annual Security Report, (ASR) and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. You may view the University’s Annual Security Report . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or you need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact: UMaine Police Department, 81 Rangeley Rd., The University of Maine, Orono, ME 04469-5794, or call 207.581.4053. Apply Now Accreditation Privacy Policy Freedom of Information Act Accessibility Title IX © 2026 University of Maine System University Students Faculty & Staff Trustees Data & Policy I am an employee Are You Still With Us? It seems you've been gone for a while. For security reasons we will end your session automatically in 03:00 unless you would like to continue working. End Session Continue Working Work Summary This summary is generated by AI Assist. Click inside the summary text box to make changes as necessary. 1000 Required Discard Add Summary Page Associate Professor or Professor of Nursing - UMS Careers loaded"
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3139": {
